1
00:00:00.567 --> 00:00:03.870
Now that you have learned tools and techniques for document processing,

2
00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.473
you'll see them in action on AWS.

3
00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:09.776
You'll learn to implement an event-driven pipeline

4
00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:12.178
that automatically triggers ADE

5
00:00:12.479 --> 00:00:15.382
to process new documents uploaded to an S3 bucket.

6
00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:20.387
These parsed documents can then be loaded into a Bedrock knowledge base,

7
00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:23.990
enabling you to answer questions through agentic RAG

8
00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.693
built with AWS Strands agents.

9
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.462
Let's get to it. Before we begin,

10
00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:31.865
I would like to thank Nneoma Okoroafor,

11
00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:35.735
Partner Solutions Architect at AWS for the collaboration.

12
00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:37.837
Here's what we will cover in this lesson. First,

13
00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:41.341
how to implement a RAG pipeline on AWS.

14
00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:45.345
Second, what we mean by Event-Driven and Serverless Architecture.

15
00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:47.781
Third, an overview of AWS resources

16
00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:50.617
you will use to implement the pipeline.

17
00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:53.019
And finally, how to build an

18
00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:58.053
agentic application with memory and knowledge base search tool with Strands Agents.

19
00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:02.962
Here's how you implemented the pre-processing phase in the previous lesson.

20
00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:05.865
First, the Raw Documents were stored locally

21
00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:08.268
and they were passed to the ADE Parser,

22
00:01:08.368 --> 00:01:11.571
which was also run locally using the computing and memory resources

23
00:01:11.671 --> 00:01:13.873
of your local environment.

24
00:01:14.174 --> 00:01:16.976
The parse outputs were then passed to the Embedder.

25
00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:19.646
We used an embedding model from OpenAI.

26
00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:22.949
And finally, the embedding vectors were passed

27
00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:25.218
to a local database using ChromaDB.

28
00:01:25.218 --> 00:01:27.287
We're now going to swap all

29
00:01:27.287 --> 00:01:29.722
of these local components with AWS services.

30
00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:33.226
This will help make this pipeline production ready on the cloud,

31
00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:36.729
which can easily scale with a larger number of documents.

32
00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.432
So, instead of storing the documents locally,

33
00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:41.434
we'll upload them to an S3 bucket,

34
00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:44.003
which is a cloud object storage.

35
00:01:44.003 --> 00:01:47.207
Instead of using the computing resources of your own machine,

36
00:01:47.207 --> 00:01:50.210
we'll use a Lambda function to run the parsing logic.

37
00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:54.814
The Lambda function provides a serverless, isolated computing environment.

38
00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:57.750
and on the cloud. It will be automatically triggered

39
00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.153
when new documents are uploaded to S3.

40
00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:03.256
And finally, for the embedder and vector database,

41
00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:05.358
we'll use Amazon Bedrock services,

42
00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:09.062
which provides serverless embeddings and knowledge bases.

43
00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:11.264
If you're unfamiliar with these resources

44
00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:15.635
or what serverless means, don't worry. I'll explain them in this video.

45
00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:19.639
You also saw how to use LangChain to define a retrieval object

46
00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:24.244
that extracts information from the vector database to answer user queries.

47
00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:26.279
You'll do the same on AWS.

48
00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:29.282
You'll build an agent using Strands Agents,

49
00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:33.286
which is an open source framework for building production ready agents

50
00:02:33.286 --> 00:02:34.988
with native AWS support.

51
00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:37.991
And you'll equip the agent with a retrieval tool

52
00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:40.093
that connects it to the knowledge base.

53
00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:42.595
You'll also use Amazon Bedrock

54
00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:45.298
to provide your agent access to an LLM

55
00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:48.368
and Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Memory,

56
00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:50.970
so you can create an agent that remembers

57
00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.473
user interactions for full context.

58
00:02:54.107 --> 00:02:56.409
So here's how you can think about your architecture.

59
00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:58.912
It has AI-based components,

60
00:02:58.912 --> 00:03:03.416
LandingAI ADE for parsing and Strands for agentic framework.

61
00:03:03.416 --> 00:03:06.419
It also has these main AWS components:

62
00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:10.723
Amazon S3, AWS Lambda, and Amazon Bedrock.

63
00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:15.723
Previously, I mentioned that AWS Lambda and Amazon Bedrock

64
00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:18.698
provides serverless services. So what does that mean?

65
00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:21.668
Serverless means two things. First, you don't

66
00:03:21.668 --> 00:03:23.870
provision or manage any servers.

67
00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:27.473
AWS handles the infrastructure and security updates.

68
00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:30.677
Second, you only pay when your code actually runs,

69
00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:32.478
not during idle time.

70
00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:37.450
This also means AWS automatically scales your application based on demand.

71
00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:40.453
Whether you have 10 concurrent users or 10,000.

72
00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:43.456
And because there's no infrastructure to configure,

73
00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:46.459
you can rapidly prototype and deploy new features.

74
00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:49.162
The second characteristic of your architecture

75
00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:52.031
is that it includes an event-driven component.

76
00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:55.034
The ADE parser will run automatically

77
00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:57.303
when a new document is uploaded to S3.

78
00:03:57.303 --> 00:04:00.506
How does that work? This is what's called Event-Driven Architecture.

79
00:04:00.506 --> 00:04:04.010
A modern design pattern where systems are decoupled

80
00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:06.713
and communicate by sending and receiving events,

81
00:04:06.713 --> 00:04:10.016
which are essentially notifications that something has happened.

82
00:04:10.016 --> 00:04:12.018
Let me break this down into three steps.

83
00:04:12.018 --> 00:04:14.120
First step is Event Producers.

84
00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.789
These are components like S3 Buckets that

85
00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:19.559
emit events when pertinent actions occur.

86
00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:22.662
In your case, when you upload a file to S3,

87
00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:24.964
it emits a file uploaded event.

88
00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:27.567
Second step, event channel or broker.

89
00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:32.071
Services like Amazon EventBridge that route these events to interested parties.

90
00:04:32.472 --> 00:04:35.008
Think of it as a notification system that

91
00:04:35.008 --> 00:04:37.577
knows who needs to be informed about what.

92
00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:42.782
There are also other messaging services on AWS like Amazon SNS,

93
00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:46.819
a pub-sub framework. and Amazon SQS for queuing,

94
00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:50.923
which support simpler pub-sub plus queuing patterns.

95
00:04:51.124 --> 00:04:56.124
EventBridge is best for event-driven architectures with more complex routing.

96
00:04:56.329 --> 00:04:58.131
Third step is Event Consumers.

97
00:04:58.331 --> 00:05:03.136
Services like AWS Lambda that subscribe to and react to specific events.

98
00:05:03.336 --> 00:05:06.139
Your Lambda function with ADE parser

99
00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:08.741
is listening for that file uploaded event

100
00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:11.811
and automatically starts processing when it arrives.

101
00:05:11.878 --> 00:05:13.680
Here's a helpful analogy.

102
00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.582
Instead of constantly polling, are we there yet?

103
00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:20.486
checking S3 every few seconds to see if there's a new file.

104
00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:23.389
An event-driven approach is like a push notification.

105
00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:27.393
S3 notifies Lambda the moment interesting events happen.

106
00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:29.595
And Lambda immediately reacts.

107
00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:33.566
So when you upload a document to S3, S3 creates an event.

108
00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:35.768
EventBridge routes it,

109
00:05:35.768 --> 00:05:39.372
and your Lambda function automatically runs the ADE parser.

110
00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:41.841
No manual trigger is needed.

111
00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:44.177
Let's now break down each component of

112
00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:46.112
your system and understand its specific role.

113
00:05:46.646 --> 00:05:49.082
You have three main AWS components:

114
00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:53.286
Amazon S3, AWS Lambda, and Amazon Bedrock.

115
00:05:53.586 --> 00:05:56.122
Let's dive into each one and see what they do.

116
00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:58.224
First up is Amazon S3,

117
00:05:58.424 --> 00:06:00.927
which stands for Simple Storage Service.

118
00:06:01.327 --> 00:06:03.830
S3 is scalable object storage service from

119
00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:06.332
AWS for files of any type and

120
00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:10.269
size. Think of it as an infinite digital filing cabinet

121
00:06:10.269 --> 00:06:12.372
accessible from anywhere on the internet.

122
00:06:12.372 --> 00:06:13.940
No matter how many files you

123
00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:15.775
add, it never runs out of space.

124
00:06:15.775 --> 00:06:18.778
This will serve as the central repository for your data.

125
00:06:18.778 --> 00:06:21.481
You store raw input data like PDFs,

126
00:06:21.481 --> 00:06:23.783
images, or text files

127
00:06:23.783 --> 00:06:25.785
in dedicated S3 buckets.

128
00:06:25.785 --> 00:06:28.488
Then you save the results of your AI processing,

129
00:06:28.488 --> 00:06:30.490
summaries, parsed documents,

130
00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:34.494
and transformed data back into organized folders.

131
00:06:34.527 --> 00:06:36.629
within those buckets. Just as a quick note,

132
00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:39.532
a bucket is the top-level container

133
00:06:39.532 --> 00:06:42.034
you create in S3 to organize your files,

134
00:06:42.034 --> 00:06:45.338
similar to a main folder on your computer. Next is AWS Lambda.

135
00:06:45.338 --> 00:06:48.541
It is a serverless compute service that runs your code

136
00:06:48.541 --> 00:06:51.344
without you managing any servers.

137
00:06:51.344 --> 00:06:54.547
Think of it as a part-time robot assistant or a function-on-demand

138
00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:57.049
that only activates when called.

139
00:06:57.049 --> 00:06:59.051
It's not running all the time.

140
00:06:59.051 --> 00:07:01.754
It springs into action only when it's needed.

141
00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:04.190
Lambda executes small pieces of code

142
00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:06.492
in response to specific events,

143
00:07:06.492 --> 00:07:08.995
like S3 uploads, API calls,

144
00:07:08.995 --> 00:07:10.696
or scheduled triggers.

145
00:07:10.997 --> 00:07:13.900
In your case, when a document is uploaded to S3,

146
00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:17.303
Lambda will automatically run your ADE parsing logic.

147
00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:19.906
And you pay only for the compute time consumed,

148
00:07:19.906 --> 00:07:21.707
measured in milliseconds.

149
00:07:21.741 --> 00:07:25.645
So if you're a Lambda function runs only for 2 milliseconds,

150
00:07:26.045 --> 00:07:28.648
you only pay for those 2 milliseconds of compute.

151
00:07:28.848 --> 00:07:32.151
Now, for Lambda to access other AWS services,

152
00:07:32.452 --> 00:07:35.655
like reading from S3, it needs appropriate permissions.

153
00:07:35.855 --> 00:07:37.857
This is where IAM comes in.

154
00:07:37.957 --> 00:07:41.060
IAM stands for identity and access management.

155
00:07:41.394 --> 00:07:43.963
is the security framework for controlling who

156
00:07:43.963 --> 00:07:46.899
or what can access your resources on AWS.

157
00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:48.835
When you create a Lambda function,

158
00:07:48.835 --> 00:07:50.603
you need to set up two things.

159
00:07:51.103 --> 00:07:54.307
An IAM Role and an IAM Policy.

160
00:07:54.574 --> 00:07:56.576
Let me explain the difference.

161
00:07:56.576 --> 00:07:58.377
Role represents who can have access.

162
00:07:58.377 --> 00:08:01.080
Think of a role as an ID badge or job title.

163
00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:04.784
It's the identity that your Lambda function assumes when it runs.

164
00:08:04.784 --> 00:08:09.388
The role proves that I am the S3-to-Bedrock-Processor function

165
00:08:09.388 --> 00:08:11.390
or I'm the document parser.

166
00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:14.594
Services like Lambda don't have usernames or passwords.

167
00:08:14.594 --> 00:08:18.097
Instead, they assume roles to prove their identity.

168
00:08:18.264 --> 00:08:20.266
to other AWS services.

169
00:08:20.366 --> 00:08:22.768
So when your Lambda function starts running,

170
00:08:22.869 --> 00:08:26.239
it tells AWS, I'm assuming the role of document processor.

171
00:08:26.339 --> 00:08:29.041
and here is my temporary access credential.

172
00:08:29.342 --> 00:08:32.044
IAM Policy, however, is what it can do.

173
00:08:32.245 --> 00:08:35.147
Now, a role by itself doesn't guarantee any permissions.

174
00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:37.049
That's where the policy comes in.

175
00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:39.752
Think of the policy as a list of rules

176
00:08:39.852 --> 00:08:41.754
written on that ID badge.

177
00:08:41.921 --> 00:08:45.024
specifies exactly what actions the role is allowed to perform.

178
00:08:45.324 --> 00:08:49.629
The policy is a JSON document that defines actions like s3:GetObject,

179
00:08:49.629 --> 00:08:53.533
which means you can read files from S3.

180
00:08:53.533 --> 00:08:55.134
Or s3:PutObject,

181
00:08:55.134 --> 00:08:57.904
which means you can write files to S3.

182
00:08:58.104 --> 00:09:00.806
Putting it all together, when you create a Lambda function,

183
00:09:00.806 --> 00:09:03.109
you have to create a IAM role,

184
00:09:03.109 --> 00:09:05.211
the identity or badge.

185
00:09:05.678 --> 00:09:08.381
attach an IAM policy to that role,

186
00:09:08.481 --> 00:09:09.916
the rules on the badge, and

187
00:09:09.916 --> 00:09:11.584
assign the role to your Lambda function.

188
00:09:11.884 --> 00:09:13.886
Hopefully the key takeaways are clear.

189
00:09:14.086 --> 00:09:18.090
Role defines who it is and policy specifies what it can do.

190
00:09:18.491 --> 00:09:21.260
Finally, Amazon Bedrock. Amazon Bedrock

191
00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:23.362
is a fully managed service that

192
00:09:23.362 --> 00:09:25.431
provides access to foundation models,

193
00:09:25.731 --> 00:09:27.433
large language models like Claude

194
00:09:27.733 --> 00:09:29.335
or Nova from AWS,

195
00:09:29.735 --> 00:09:31.337
along with embedding models,

196
00:09:31.337 --> 00:09:33.039
all through a single API.

197
00:09:33.439 --> 00:09:36.742
Think of it as a menu of pre-trained AI models.

198
00:09:36.909 --> 00:09:39.912
Instead of training and hosting your own models from scratch,

199
00:09:39.912 --> 00:09:42.715
you can simply select the model you need and use it.

200
00:09:43.115 --> 00:09:45.318
Amazon Bedrock powers three key parts

201
00:09:45.318 --> 00:09:47.920
of your architecture. First, the Knowledge Base.

202
00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:50.222
This is where your parsed documents

203
00:09:50.222 --> 00:09:52.725
are automatically embedded and stored as vectors.

204
00:09:53.125 --> 00:09:54.894
Bedrock handles the embedding process,

205
00:09:55.094 --> 00:09:58.397
converting your text into a numerical representation and

206
00:09:58.831 --> 00:10:01.033
providing semantic search capabilities

207
00:10:01.033 --> 00:10:04.036
so your agent can retrieve relevant information.

208
00:10:04.537 --> 00:10:06.238
Second, Agent Runtime.

209
00:10:06.439 --> 00:10:08.441
Bedrock provides the foundational model

210
00:10:08.441 --> 00:10:11.043
that powers your agent's reasoning and responses.

211
00:10:11.444 --> 00:10:13.245
When your agent receives a query,

212
00:10:13.546 --> 00:10:15.748
it uses Bedrock's LLM to generate

213
00:10:16.048 --> 00:10:18.150
intelligent, grounded answers.

214
00:10:18.351 --> 00:10:20.152
Third, AgentCore Memory.

215
00:10:20.786 --> 00:10:23.089
This is where your agent's memory is stored.

216
00:10:23.389 --> 00:10:27.360
Conversation history, user preferences, and semantic facts.

217
00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:31.664
Memory allows your agent to remember context across interactions,

218
00:10:32.064 --> 00:10:34.367
making it feel more like a real assistant.

219
00:10:34.667 --> 00:10:37.370
Keep in mind that Bedrock is serverless in nature,

220
00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:41.374
so it scales automatically, and you only pay for what you use.

221
00:10:41.807 --> 00:10:45.578
Now, let's go through the agentic component of your architecture.

222
00:10:45.778 --> 00:10:48.581
Strands Agents. It's an open-source SDK.

223
00:10:48.981 --> 00:10:52.785
Strands Agent is an AWS open-source framework

224
00:10:52.785 --> 00:10:56.589
specifically designed for building agents in notebooks and production environments.

225
00:10:56.989 --> 00:10:58.991
It helps simplify orchestration.

226
00:10:59.191 --> 00:11:02.795
Strands Agent seamlessly integrates with AWS resources

227
00:11:02.795 --> 00:11:05.598
such as S3, Bedrock, and other tools

228
00:11:05.598 --> 00:11:09.802
without requiring complex manual coding. It also helps create agent

229
00:11:09.969 --> 00:11:14.173
definition. With Strands Agent, you specify which Bedrock models to use,

230
00:11:14.373 --> 00:11:17.576
what tools your agent can access, and how memory works.

231
00:11:17.777 --> 00:11:22.777
This makes your agent configuration clear, maintainable, and easy to modify.

232
00:11:23.182 --> 00:11:26.152
Last but not least, it is also Enterprise Ready.

233
00:11:26.352 --> 00:11:28.754
Strands Agent is production-grade.

234
00:11:28.954 --> 00:11:31.857
It comes with built-in tooling for tracing

235
00:11:31.857 --> 00:11:34.760
and logging, performance monitoring, and error handling.

236
00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:37.763
And it also supports flexible deployment patterns.

237
00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:40.633
Now let's see how all these components work together.

238
00:11:40.633 --> 00:11:42.635
Let's walk through the process step by step.

239
00:11:42.935 --> 00:11:44.637
one is uploading the document.

240
00:11:44.937 --> 00:11:47.940
In the lab, you'll work with medical research papers.

241
00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:52.344
You upload a PDF to the S3 input medical folder.

242
00:11:52.745 --> 00:11:55.648
The S3 upload event triggers your Lambda function.

243
00:11:55.948 --> 00:12:00.352
Lambda runs the ADE parser to parse and structure the content.

244
00:12:00.653 --> 00:12:03.656
The parsed output is uploaded back to S3

245
00:12:03.656 --> 00:12:06.325
in the output medical folder.

246
00:12:06.325 --> 00:12:09.628
in two formats: a markdown file for the parsed content

247
00:12:09.628 --> 00:12:13.799
and a JSON file containing chunk information for visual grounding,

248
00:12:13.799 --> 00:12:16.502
like chunk type and bounding box coordinates.

249
00:12:16.502 --> 00:12:18.404
This all happens automatically.

250
00:12:18.404 --> 00:12:20.439
You just upload the file and

251
00:12:20.439 --> 00:12:22.808
event-driven architecture takes care of the rest.

252
00:12:23.109 --> 00:12:26.912
Step two is ingesting the parsed documents into your knowledge base.

253
00:12:26.912 --> 00:12:31.912
You start ingesting the markdown files from the S3 output medical folder

254
00:12:32.218 --> 00:12:34.520
into the knowledge base. Amazon Bedrock

255
00:12:34.587 --> 00:12:38.357
reads the files, generates the embeddings for each chunk of text,

256
00:12:38.758 --> 00:12:40.960
and stores them in a vector database.

257
00:12:41.393 --> 00:12:44.663
Once the ingestion is complete, your knowledge base is now searchable.

258
00:12:45.064 --> 00:12:48.667
The agent can query this knowledge base to retrieve relevant information.

259
00:12:49.034 --> 00:12:51.370
Note that you can also implement a

260
00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:53.706
separate Lambda function for the ingestion job

261
00:12:54.006 --> 00:12:57.009
from S3 buckets to the knowledge base automatically.

262
00:12:57.276 --> 00:12:59.345
But in the lab, we'll keep it simple

263
00:12:59.578 --> 00:13:02.381
and only implement one Lambda function for parsing.

264
00:13:02.648 --> 00:13:06.051
Step three is creating the search_knowledge_base tool.

265
00:13:06.252 --> 00:13:08.854
This tool connects your agent to the knowledge base.

266
00:13:08.954 --> 00:13:12.558
When the agent needs information from your documents, it calls this tool,

267
00:13:13.159 --> 00:13:17.062
which queries the vector database and returns the most relevant content.

268
00:13:17.263 --> 00:13:19.665
This is what enables your agent to

269
00:13:19.665 --> 00:13:22.067
answer questions based on your uploaded documents.

270
00:13:22.468 --> 00:13:24.670
Step four is setting up the agent memory.

271
00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:26.872
Amazon Bedrock AgentCore

272
00:13:26.939 --> 00:13:31.939
memory provides three types of Long-Term Memory. One is User Preference.

273
00:13:32.144 --> 00:13:35.548
It stores likes, dislikes, and personal context.

274
00:13:35.848 --> 00:13:40.553
Second is Semantic Memory, which stores facts, entities, and relationships.

275
00:13:41.053 --> 00:13:45.524
Third is Summary Memory, which stores conversation summaries and key points.

276
00:13:46.125 --> 00:13:48.727
Importantly, memory will persist across sessions,

277
00:13:48.727 --> 00:13:50.930
so your agent can remember past

278
00:13:51.197 --> 00:13:53.999
interactions and provide personalized responses.

279
00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:56.502
Step five is building the agent itself.

280
00:13:56.602 --> 00:13:58.404
You will configure the system prompt,

281
00:13:58.404 --> 00:14:00.506
instructions for how the agent should behave,

282
00:14:00.606 --> 00:14:03.209
the search knowledge base tool you created,

283
00:14:03.309 --> 00:14:06.212
the memory, enabled by AgentCore Memory,

284
00:14:06.312 --> 00:14:09.014
and the LLM model hosted on Bedrock.

285
00:14:09.215 --> 00:14:11.150
Once everything is configured, your agent

286
00:14:11.150 --> 00:14:13.419
is ready to interact with the users.

287
00:14:13.519 --> 00:14:15.487
Finally, step six.

288
00:14:15.487 --> 00:14:17.590
You can chat with your agent.

289
00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:19.592
Here is an example.

290
00:14:19.592 --> 00:14:21.994
The user might say, I like sushi with tuna.

291
00:14:21.994 --> 00:14:25.497
And the agent might respond, Got it! I'll remember that preference.

292
00:14:25.497 --> 00:14:27.533
And in the later session, you might

293
00:14:27.533 --> 00:14:29.869
ask, What should I eat for lunch today?

294
00:14:29.869 --> 00:14:31.670
The agent might respond, How about

295
00:14:31.670 --> 00:14:33.772
sushi? You mentioned that you like tuna.

296
00:14:33.772 --> 00:14:36.775
The key feature is that the agent remembers you.

297
00:14:37.376 --> 00:14:40.679
It's not just answering isolated questions in one session.

298
00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:44.383
It's building context and learning your preferences over time.

299
00:14:44.884 --> 00:14:48.187
Now it's lab time. Let's switch to the notebook and start building.
